{
  "result": "There is a specific kind of silence that only exists at 4 a.m. It's not empty, but full. The refrigerator hums, the house settles, and somewhere outside a distant train sighs against the tracks. Most of the world is asleep, and in that collective pause, your thoughts finally have room to stretch out. I think we don't get enough of that kind of spaciousness anymore. Our days are crowded with notifications, headlines, half-finished conversations and the low hum of anxiety that comes from being perpetually connected. We scroll not because we want to, but because stillness has started to feel uncomfortable, almost like forgetting how to be alone with ourselves. And yet stillness is where ideas are born. Every book you love, every song that made you cry, every invention that changed the world began as a quiet thought in someone's mind, a small spark that needed silence to catch fire. I like to imagine thoughts as seeds. Some need constant attention and watering, others need to be left alone in the dark to germinate. You never know which is which until you try. Maybe the point of writing, of art, of living attentively, is simply to create a little greenhouse for those seeds, a place where they can become whatever they were meant to be. So tonight, if you can, turn off the lights, close the tabs, and just sit with the quiet for five minutes. See what grows. You might be surprised what was waiting there all along.",
